/* Week 9 — Leadership (Ch 9, 13th ed.). Ch 12 (Power, Politics & Ethics) is the week's other chapter but is not on Assignment #2. */
SME.addWeek({
  id: 'w9', num: 9, title: 'Leadership', dates: 'Nov 2 – Nov 6',
  chapterLine: 'Ch 9',
  chapters: [
    { c: 'Ch 9', t: 'Leadership', s: '§9.1–9.10', note: 'Ch 12 (Power, Politics & Ethics) is also taught in Week 9 but is not on Assignment #2, so it is left out of this page' },
    { c: 'Slides', t: 'Week 9A (29 slides, Ch 9 only)', s: 'formal vs informal · leaders vs managers · slide 8 map · Fiedler · path–goal · Vroom–Jago · slide lists · Leadership Effectiveness Equation (L1 + L2 + GM + S)' }
  ],
  sources: ['Textbook Ch 9, §9.1–9.10 (13th ed.)', 'Week 9A deck (29 slides)', 'Lesson page /1BA3/ch9 (the fact-checked source for this page)'],
  keys: ['Formal vs emergent', 'Trait theory', 'Four leader behaviours', 'Fiedler & LPC', 'Octants', 'Path–goal', 'Vroom–Jago', 'LMX', 'Transformational', 'Positive leadership', 'L1 + L2 + GM + S'],

  brief: [
    '<b>Leadership</b> is the influence particular individuals exert on the goal achievement of others in an organizational context. Know the three kinds: <b>strategic</b> (steering the organization’s future), <b>formal / assigned</b> (comes with a role) and <b>emergent</b> (no title, seen as a leader). The slide’s <b>“informal” = the text’s “emergent.”</b> The chapter is a tour of theories that each answer one question: who leads well (<b>traits</b>), what leaders do (<b>behaviours</b>), when each style works (<b>situational</b>), how much to involve people (<b>participation</b>), and what kind of relationship to build (<b>LMX, transactional, transformational, positive</b>).',
    'The lecture’s organizing map (slide 8) sorts the theories on two axes: <b>trait (innate) vs behaviour (can be trained)</b> and <b>universal vs situation-contingent</b>. Universal-trait: trait theory and leadership categorization. Universal-behaviour: consideration / initiating structure and transformational. Contingent-trait: <b>Fiedler</b> (LPC is a trait). Contingent-behaviour: <b>path–goal</b> and <b>Vroom–Jago</b>. The text’s four leader behaviours are <b>consideration, initiating structure, leader reward and leader punishment</b> (care, control, carrot, cane).',
    '<b>Fiedler:</b> measure the leader (<b>high LPC = relationship oriented, low LPC = task oriented</b>) and the situation (<b>leader–member relations, task structure, position power</b>, giving eight octants). <b>Task (low LPC) wins at the favourable and unfavourable ends; relationship (high LPC) wins in the middle.</b> <b>Path–goal (House):</b> four behaviours (<b>directive, supportive, participative, achievement-oriented</b>) matched to <b>employee characteristics</b> and <b>environmental factors</b> to raise satisfaction, acceptance of the leader and effort. <b>Vroom–Jago:</b> five strategies (AI, AII, CI, CII, GII) chosen by a decision tree on quality and commitment.',
    '<b>Relationship theories:</b> <b>LMX</b> (a unique exchange with each employee; high vs low quality), <b>transactional</b> (contingent reward + management by exception) and <b>transformational</b> (intellectual stimulation, individualized consideration, inspirational motivation, charisma; charisma most important). The best leaders are both. <b>Positive leadership</b> adds empowering, ethical, authentic and servant leadership. The chapter ends with gender (glass ceiling vs labyrinth, role congruity), the GLOBE project and global leadership. Its closing answer to “what style is best?” is <b>“it depends.”</b>',
    'The lecture adds the <b>Leadership Effectiveness Equation: Leader Effectiveness = L1 (leader traits) + L2 (leader behaviours) + GM (group member characteristics) + S (situation, internal and external)</b>. It is lecture-only and works as a one-line summary of the whole chapter. Watch the slide-vs-text traps: “initiating consideration,” path–goal’s “authoritarianism / locus of control / formal authority,” and slide headings (developmental leadership, change leadership, sustainability) that the 13th-ed chapter never explains.'
  ],
  split: 'If two people share Week 9: <b>SME A: Who leads and what leaders do</b> (§9.1–9.3 and the slide 8 map: leadership types, trait theory, the four behaviours, plus the Leadership Effectiveness Equation). <b>SME B: When and how</b> (§9.4–9.10: Fiedler, path–goal, Vroom–Jago, LMX, transactional / transformational, positive leadership, gender / culture / global). <b>Both</b> should know the <b>slide 8 map</b> and the “it depends” conclusion, because the situational theories are where scenario questions are won. Ch 12 (Power, Politics & Ethics) is not on Assignment #2 and is not on this page.',
  lens: 'Three classic setups. <b>“Use path–goal to explain how your style would differ in each situation”:</b> read the employees (need for achievement, preference for direction, perceived ability) and the environment (clear routine task, challenging ambiguous task, frustrating job), pick the behaviour, and say why. <b>A leader and a situation described in detail (Fiedler):</b> classify the three factors, name the octant, state the favourability and the predicted orientation (task at the ends, relationship in the middle). <b>A behaviour described and you name the dimension or style:</b> “challenges assumptions” (intellectual stimulation), “coaches each person” (individualized consideration), “rewards goal attainment” (contingent reward), “asks for input then decides” (CI / CII). Always finish with “it depends” and a recommendation.',

  sections: [
    /* ─────────────── LEADERSHIP BASICS & THE MAP ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'basics', title: 'What leadership is & the chapter map', ref: '§9.1 · slides 2–8',
      lede: 'Week 9A, slides 2–8. Chapter 9 is taught with Ch 12, but only Ch 9 is on Assignment #2.',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Leadership', ref: '§9.1 · slide 2', def: 'Leadership occurs when particular individuals <b>exert influence on the goal achievement of others in an organizational context</b>. Effective leadership uses that influence to achieve organizational goals by enhancing productivity, innovation, satisfaction and commitment. It is about motivating people and gaining their commitment about what needs to be done and how.',
          after: 'Slide 2 adds two <b>lecture-only</b> quotes: Kouzes and Posner (“the art of mobilizing others to want to struggle for shared aspirations,” so followers <i>want</i> to act) and Warren Buffett (success needs integrity, intelligence and energy; without the first, the other two wreck you).' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Strategic, formal and emergent leadership', ref: '§9.1 · slide 3', head: ['Type', 'What it is', 'Key point'],
          rows: [['<b>Strategic</b>', 'Ability to anticipate, envision, stay flexible, think strategically and work with others to create a viable future for the organization', 'Exercised from the top; gives sustainable competitive advantage in turbulent environments'], ['<b>Formal (assigned)</b>', 'Leadership that comes with a formal role (manager, executive, supervisor)', 'Expected to lead and given authority, but a title does not guarantee influence. The chapter focuses on this one'], ['<b>Emergent (informal)</b>', 'Degree to which someone with <b>no formal status</b> is seen by team members as exhibiting leaderlike influence', 'Relies on being well liked or seen as highly skilled']] },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Shared leadership', ref: '§9.1 · slide 19', def: 'An <b>emergent and dynamic team phenomenon</b> in which leadership roles and influence are distributed among team members, with different members taking the lead at different times. The more leadership is shared, the better the team performs and the more creative it is.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Informal = emergent (and neither is shared or strategic)', body: 'Slide 3 says <b>formal vs informal</b>; the text says <b>formal (assigned) vs emergent</b> and itself calls emergent leadership “informal leadership roles.” Same idea, two labels. <b>Emergent</b> = one person without a title seen as a leader; <b>shared</b> = influence spread across several members over time; <b>strategic</b> = steering the organization’s future. Slide 3 also says informal leaders have “<b>positive power always</b>”; the text never says that. Emergent leaders may not always support organizational goals, so treat “always” as slide shorthand, not an exam fact.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Leaders vs managers (lecture-only)', src: 'lec', ref: 'slides 4–5', head: ['Aspect', 'Leaders', 'Managers'],
          rows: [['Vision', 'Develop and communicate a vision', 'Implement and manage plans'], ['Motivation', 'Inspire people to engage with the vision', 'Maintain processes, ensure compliance'], ['Change', 'Embrace and drive change', 'Manage stability, minimize disruption'], ['Risk', 'Willing to take risks for innovation', 'Mitigate risk, follow procedures'], ['Relationships', 'Build personal connections and trust', 'Establish formal authority and structure']],
          after: 'Slide 4 asks “Is it possible to be a manager yet not have influence?” The text answers yes: some managers “fail to exert any influence” and are judged ineffective leaders. The chapter itself never compares leaders with managers. Treat the table as tendencies, not rules.',
          mnem: '<b>Leaders:</b> <b>Look</b> ahead, <b>Light</b> the fire, <b>Lead</b> change, <b>Leap</b> (risk), <b>Link</b> with people. <b>Managers:</b> <b>Make</b> plans happen, <b>Maintain</b> processes, <b>Keep</b> stable, <b>Mitigate</b> risk, <b>Structure</b> and authority.' },
        { t: 'groups', title: 'The chapter map: trait vs behaviour × universal vs contingent (lecture)', src: 'lec', ref: 'slide 8',
          intro: 'Dr. Kassaye’s grid for sorting the theories. One axis: <b>trait (innate)</b> vs <b>behaviour (can be trained)</b>. The other: <b>universal</b> (same leadership works everywhere) vs <b>situation-contingent</b> (depends on the setting).',
          groups: [
            { name: 'Universal · Trait', items: ['Trait theory (slide 9)', 'Leadership categorization theory'] },
            { name: 'Universal · Behaviour', items: ['Consideration and initiating structure (slide 10)', 'Transformational leadership'] },
            { name: 'Contingent · Trait', items: ['<b>Fiedler’s contingency model</b> (LPC is treated as a personality trait)'] },
            { name: 'Contingent · Behaviour', items: ['<b>House’s path–goal</b>', '<b>Vroom–Jago</b> participation model'] }
          ],
          after: 'LMX, the positive styles, gender and culture are <b>not</b> on the slide’s grid. The grid is lecture framing; the text does not draw it. “Innate” is the slide’s simplification: the text says some traits (e.g. conscientiousness) can rise after people become leaders.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'info', title: 'Chapter 12 is left out of this page', body: 'Week 9 also covers <b>Ch 12 (Power, Politics and Ethics)</b>. It is not on Assignment #2 and has no lesson page yet, so it is not covered here. A few lecture touches (slide 7’s “personal sources of power”) point to it; leave those for Ch 12.' }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── TRAITS ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'traits', title: 'Trait theory', ref: '§9.2 · slide 9',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Trait theory of leadership', ref: '§9.2 · slide 9', def: 'Leadership depends on the <b>personal qualities (traits)</b> of the leader: physical characteristics, intellectual ability and personality. Leaders are assumed to have a special set of traits that distinguish them from followers. The slide frames this as the belief that certain people are destined to lead <b>regardless of the situation</b>.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Exhibit 9.1: nine traits related to leadership', ref: '§9.2 · Exhibit 9.1',
          intro: 'Leaders tend to be higher than average, though the links are not strong. Together: a high-energy person who wants impact, but is smart and stable enough not to abuse power.',
          items: ['<b>Intelligence</b>', '<b>Energy and drive</b>', '<b>Self-confidence</b>', '<b>Dominance</b>', '<b>Motivation to lead</b>', '<b>Emotional stability</b>', '<b>Honesty and integrity</b>', '<b>Need for achievement</b>', '<b>Sociability</b>'],
          after: 'Big Five: all five relate to leadership, but <b>extraversion and conscientiousness</b> are the most consistent predictors. <b>Narcissism</b> is linked to leadership but as a risk (bullying, fraud, volatile performance), not an effectiveness trait. Demographic traits (age, gender, height, education) are mostly unrelated.',
          mnem: '<b>“I Enjoy Strong Direction; My Early Heroes Need Sleep”</b>: Intelligence · Energy · Self-confidence · Dominance · Motivation to lead · Emotional stability · Honesty · Need for achievement · Sociability.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Three types of motivation to lead (MTL)', ref: '§9.2 · Research Focus',
          items: ['<b>Affective-identity</b>: you enjoy leading or see yourself as a leader. The <b>strongest link</b> to leadership outcomes; the only type that predicts more effective leaders.', '<b>Social-normative</b>: a sense of duty or obligation. The only type positively related to <b>transactional</b> leadership.', '<b>Noncalculative</b>: you do not weigh the costs and benefits of leading.'],
          after: 'All three are positively related to emergent and transformational leadership and negatively to laissez-faire. Cue: love it, owe it, don’t count the cost.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Four limitations of the trait approach', ref: '§9.2 · slide 9',
          items: ['<b>Cause and effect unclear</b>: do traits make the leader, or does leading change the traits?', '<b>Does not say what leaders should do</b> (little guidance on training or diagnosing failure).', '<b>Invites bias and discrimination</b>.', '<b>Ignores the situation</b>, which the text calls the most crucial problem.'],
          after: 'Slide 9 lists three limits in its own words: overemphasis on traits, potential for bias and “static nature.” The text does not say traits never change, so do not claim that.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Leadership categorization theory', ref: '§9.2 · slide 9', def: 'People hold a <b>prototype</b> of what a leader is like and are more likely to see someone as a leader, and an effective one, when they match it. Evidence: leaders (not employees) are more often assumed to be white, and white leaders are rated more effective when credited with success (the “white-leader effect”).' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Trait theory vs categorization theory', body: 'Trait theory is a claim about <b>the leader</b> (they have certain qualities). Categorization theory is a claim about <b>the perceivers</b> (they judge people against a prototype) and is a criticism of relying on traits. A stem about intelligence or extraversion is trait theory; a stem about whom raters assume to be a leader is categorization. The text’s bottom line: traits are at most a precondition, and <b>behaviours matter more and can be learned</b>.' }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── BEHAVIOURS ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'behaviours', title: 'The behaviour of leaders', ref: '§9.3 · slide 10',
      items: [
        { t: 'groups', title: 'The four leader behaviours', ref: '§9.3',
          intro: 'Ohio State (1940s) found two basic behaviours; the text adds two more. They are not opposites: a leader can be high on both.',
          groups: [
            { name: 'Consideration (Care)', items: ['Approachable; shows personal concern and respect for employees', 'Friendly, egalitarian, appreciative, supportive, protective of group welfare'] },
            { name: 'Initiating structure (Control)', items: ['Concentrates on <b>group goal attainment</b>', 'Defines roles, stresses standard procedures, schedules work, assigns tasks'] },
            { name: 'Leader reward behaviour (Carrot)', items: ['Compliments, tangible benefits, deserved special treatment', 'Strong results when <b>contingent</b> on performance'] },
            { name: 'Leader punishment behaviour (Cane)', items: ['Reprimands, unfavourable assignments, withheld raises or promotions', 'Helps only when contingent; <b>non-contingent punishment</b> brings dissatisfaction'] }
          ],
          mnem: '<b>Care, Control, Carrot, Cane.</b> The shared exam point for the last two: they work only when <b>contingent on behaviour</b>.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Consequences of the behaviours (answering slide 10’s “Consequences?”)', ref: '§9.3 · slide 10',
          items: ['Both consideration and initiating structure contribute positively to motivation, satisfaction and effectiveness.', '<b>Consideration</b> is more strongly related to follower satisfaction (with leader and job), motivation and leader effectiveness.', '<b>Initiating structure</b> is slightly more strongly related to leader job performance and group performance.', 'Contingent reward: trust in supervisor, satisfaction, commitment, effort, performance, OCB. Contingent punishment goes with better outcomes; non-contingent with worse.', 'Which matters more depends on the task, the employee and the setting, which leads to the situational theories.'] },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Slide 10: “initiating consideration” is not a term', body: 'The textbook behaviour is simply <b>consideration</b>. “Initiating consideration” (slides 10 and 25, “initiates consideration”) mixes up the slide’s own labels; “initiating <i>structure</i>” is correct. “Task leader” and “social-emotional leader” are lecture labels, not in the 13th-ed chapter, and the slide’s “reducing tension, resolving disagreements, maintaining morale” is its own wording. The slide also shows only <b>two of the text’s four</b> behaviours. On the exam write <b>consideration</b>.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Consideration vs individualized consideration', body: '<b>Consideration</b> (Ohio State) is a style toward the <b>whole group</b>: approachable, concerned, respectful. <b>Individualized consideration</b> is one of the four <b>transformational</b> dimensions: treating each employee as a distinct individual and coaching or mentoring them <b>one-on-one</b>. Path–goal’s <b>supportive</b> behaviour is essentially identical to Ohio State consideration, and <b>directive</b> to initiating structure.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Leader reward behaviour vs contingent reward behaviour', body: '<b>Leader reward behaviour</b> is the broad category (compliments, benefits, special treatment). <b>Contingent reward behaviour</b> is that reward made <b>dependent on meeting performance goals</b>, and it is the form that produces strong results. It is also the first of the two behaviours that define <b>transactional</b> leadership, so it is not a new idea in the transactional section. The same logic applies to punishment: contingent vs non-contingent decides whether it helps or hurts.' }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── FIEDLER ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'fiedler', title: 'Fiedler’s contingency theory', ref: '§9.4 · slides 11–13',
      lede: 'The first situational theory. Two parts: measure the leader’s orientation, then measure the situation. The slide files it under “contingency / TRAIT” because LPC reveals a personality trait.',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Least preferred co-worker (LPC) scale', ref: '§9.4 · slide 12', def: 'The leader describes the <b>current or past co-worker with whom they had the most difficulty getting the job done</b>. A <b>favourable</b> description (<b>high LPC</b>) means <b>relationship oriented</b> (despite the difficulty, they find good qualities in the person). An <b>unfavourable</b> description (<b>low LPC</b>) means <b>task oriented</b> (“no good at the job, so no good, period”).',
          after: 'Fiedler argues LPC reveals a <b>personality trait</b> reflecting the leader’s motivational structure. The text is explicit that LPC is an <b>attitude</b> toward work relationships, not a measure of the behaviours consideration or initiating structure.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'High LPC = relationship, Low LPC = task (reversed)', body: 'A high score sounds like “high task focus” but means the opposite: <b>high LPC = relationship oriented</b>, <b>low LPC = task oriented</b>. Cue: kind to the difficult colleague = kind-hearted = relationship. Also keep <b>Fiedler’s relationship-oriented leader</b> (an LPC attitude) separate from slide 25’s <b>relationship-oriented behaviours</b> (what a leader does).' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Situational favourableness: three factors', ref: '§9.4 · slide 12',
          items: ['<b>Leader–member relations</b>: is the relationship between leader and group good or poor?', '<b>Task structure</b>: is the task highly structured or unstructured?', '<b>Position power</b>: has the organization given the leader formal authority (strong) or not (weak)?'],
          after: 'Most favourable: good relations, structured task, strong power. Least favourable: poor relations, unstructured task, weak power.',
          mnem: '<b>Relations, Task, Power</b> (the order of the exhibit). <b>Task at the tails, Relationship in the middle.</b>' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'The eight octants and the predictions (Exhibit 9.2)', ref: '§9.4 · slide 13', head: ['Octant', 'Relations', 'Task', 'Power', 'Favourability', 'Best orientation'],
          rows: [['I', 'Good', 'Structured', 'Strong', 'Very favourable', '<b>Task (low LPC)</b>'], ['II', 'Good', 'Structured', 'Weak', 'Very favourable', '<b>Task (low LPC)</b>, but evidence contradicts it'], ['III', 'Good', 'Unstructured', 'Strong', 'Very favourable', '<b>Task (low LPC)</b>'], ['IV', 'Good', 'Unstructured', 'Weak', 'Medium', '<b>Relationship (high LPC)</b>'], ['V', 'Poor', 'Structured', 'Strong', 'Medium', '<b>Relationship (high LPC)</b>'], ['VI', 'Poor', 'Structured', 'Weak', 'Medium', '<b>Relationship (high LPC)</b>'], ['VII', 'Poor', 'Unstructured', 'Strong', 'Medium', '<b>Relationship (high LPC)</b>'], ['VIII', 'Poor', 'Unstructured', 'Weak', 'Very unfavourable', '<b>Task (low LPC)</b>']],
          after: '<b>Why:</b> when the situation is favourable, employees are “ready” to be influenced and the leader can get away with a task orientation. When it is very unfavourable, task orientation is needed to get anything done. In the middle, some mix of an unclear task or poor relationship means a relationship orientation helps make the best of a stressful but workable situation. <b>Evaluation:</b> reasonable support, but the evidence contradicts the task-leadership prescription for <b>octant II</b>.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Fiedler predictions by octant: classify before you predict', body: 'Fix the three factors first, then read the octant: <b>I–III and VIII = task (low LPC); IV–VII = relationship (high LPC)</b>. The most common slip is treating “most favourable” and “least favourable” alike or forgetting that <b>octant III</b> (good relations, unstructured task, strong power) is still very favourable, while <b>octant IV</b> (same, but weak power) drops to medium and flips the prediction. Also note Fiedler changes the <b>situation to fit the leader</b>, because LPC is treated as a trait, not something to retrain.' }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── PATH-GOAL ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'pathgoal', title: 'House’s path–goal theory', ref: '§9.4 · slides 14–16 · Exhibit 9.3',
      lede: 'Robert House (building on Martin Evans). Concerned with the situations under which various leader behaviours are most effective. Slide 14 gives two steps: (1) leader behaviour, (2) situational factors.',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Path–goal theory: the core idea', ref: '§9.4 · slide 14', def: 'The most important thing leaders do is <b>clarify the paths to goals that matter to employees</b> (a promotion, a sense of accomplishment, a pleasant work climate), which should promote <b>job satisfaction, leader acceptance and effort</b>. To promote effort, leaders make rewards depend on performance and show how to earn them, which may take direction, guidance and coaching. Leader behaviour employees see as unnecessary or unhelpful is resented.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Four leader behaviours', ref: '§9.4 · slide 15', head: ['Behaviour', 'What the leader does', 'Link to earlier model'],
          rows: [['<b>Directive</b>', 'Schedules work, maintains performance standards, lets employees know what is expected', 'Essentially identical to initiating structure'], ['<b>Supportive</b>', 'Friendly, approachable, concerned with pleasant interpersonal relationships', 'Essentially identical to consideration'], ['<b>Participative</b>', 'Consults employees on work matters and considers their opinions', 'New'], ['<b>Achievement-oriented</b>', 'Encourages high effort and goal accomplishment, expresses confidence employees can reach goals', 'New']],
          mnem: '<b>Do, Soothe, Poll, Aim high</b>: directive = “do this,” supportive = soothe, participative = poll for input, achievement-oriented = aim high with confidence.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Situational factors and the best-fitting behaviour (Exhibit 9.3)', ref: '§9.4', head: ['Situational factor', 'What the text says', 'Fits'],
          rows: [['<b>Employee:</b> high need for achievement', 'Should work well under achievement-oriented leadership', 'Achievement-oriented'], ['<b>Employee:</b> prefers being told what to do', 'Responds best to a directive style', 'Directive'], ['<b>Employee:</b> low perceived task ability', 'Appreciates directive leadership and coaching; if they feel quite capable, it is unnecessary and irritating', 'Directive, only when ability is low'], ['<b>Environment:</b> clear, routine tasks (common at lower levels)', 'Directive leadership is redundant and lowers satisfaction and leader acceptance; participation is not useful either', 'Neither directive nor participative'], ['<b>Environment:</b> challenging, ambiguous tasks (common at higher levels)', 'Employees appreciate both; they clarify the path and show concern', 'Directive and participative'], ['<b>Environment:</b> frustrating, dissatisfying jobs', 'Support compensates for a disliked job (though little effect on effort)', 'Supportive']],
          after: 'The lesson: effective leaders take advantage of the motivating and satisfying parts of a job while offsetting the demotivating or dissatisfying parts. The theory predicts job satisfaction and acceptance of the leader better than it predicts performance.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Path–goal exam routine: reading a situation', ref: '§9.4 · study method',
          intro: 'A working checklist (this page’s own method, built from the exhibit):',
          items: ['<b>Employees:</b> how capable do they see themselves? Do they want direction? Are they high achievers?', '<b>Environment:</b> is the task clear and routine, or challenging and ambiguous? Is the job frustrating?', '<b>Pick the behaviour</b> that fills a gap (clarify the path, offset a bad job) and <b>avoid the one that would be redundant</b>.', '<b>Name the outcome</b> you are protecting: satisfaction, leader acceptance, effort.', 'Say how the behaviour should <b>change as the situation changes</b> (e.g. as ability grows).'] },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Slide 16: path–goal employee traits and factors that are not in the 13th ed.', body: 'Slide 16 lists employee characteristics <b>authoritarianism, locus of control and ability</b>, and environmental factors <b>nature of task and formal authority</b>. The 13th-ed text’s examples are <b>need for achievement, preference for being told what to do and perceived task ability</b> (ability is the only overlap), and environmental factors are the nature of the task and frustrating or dissatisfying jobs. It never uses “authoritarianism” or “locus of control” here and never names “formal authority” as a factor. Only two classes exist: employee characteristics and environmental factors. If a question asks for the textbook’s factors, use its examples.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Path–goal’s participative behaviour vs Vroom–Jago participation', body: 'In path–goal, <b>participative</b> is one of four behaviours (consult employees and consider their opinions), right in some situations and wrong in others. In Vroom–Jago, participation is a <b>range of five decision strategies (AI to GII)</b> and the model tells you <i>how much</i> to use for a given decision. Path–goal asks “which of four behaviours fits this employee and task?”; Vroom–Jago asks “how much should the group decide this problem?”' }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── PARTICIPATION ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'participation', title: 'Participative leadership & Vroom–Jago', ref: '§9.5 · slides 17–18',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Participative leadership', ref: '§9.5 · slide 17', def: '<b>Involving employees in making work-related decisions.</b> It is a relative concept: at the minimum, getting employee opinions before deciding; at the maximum, letting employees decide within agreed limits. It must not be confused with <b>abdication</b> of leadership, which is almost always ineffective. Exhibit 9.4 (Tannenbaum and Schmidt) runs from <b>boss-centred</b> (high use of authority) to <b>employee-centred</b> (large area of freedom).' },
        { t: 'groups', title: 'Advantages and problems of participation', ref: '§9.5 · slide 17',
          groups: [
            { name: 'Advantages', items: ['<b>Motivation and performance</b>: goal setting, variety, autonomy; more satisfaction, performance, OCB', '<b>Quality</b>: “two heads are better than one,” especially if employees hold technical knowledge', '<b>Acceptance</b>: higher acceptance of decisions, especially on fairness issues (vacation schedules)'] },
            { name: 'Problems', items: ['<b>Time and energy</b>: wrong when a quick decision is needed (the emergency room)', '<b>Loss of power</b>: insecure leaders fear it and hand over trivial choices', '<b>Lack of receptivity or knowledge</b>: employees may resent “management’s work” or lack information on constraints'] }
          ] },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Vroom–Jago’s five strategies', ref: '§9.5 · slide 18', head: ['Strategy', 'What the leader does', 'Employees’ role'],
          rows: [['<b>AI</b>', 'Decides alone with information at hand', 'None'], ['<b>AII</b>', 'Gets necessary information from employees, then decides alone', 'Information providers only'], ['<b>CI</b>', 'Shares the problem with relevant employees <i>individually</i>, then decides', 'Advisers, one at a time'], ['<b>CII</b>', 'Shares the problem with employees <i>as a group</i>, then decides', 'Advisers, as a group'], ['<b>GII</b>', 'Group generates and evaluates alternatives and seeks consensus; leader chairs and accepts the group’s solution', 'Joint decision makers']],
          after: 'A = autocratic, C = consultative, G = group; I = an individual, II = a group involved.',
          mnem: '<b>Who decides? A, A, C, C, G.</b> Ladder of employee influence: none, information, individual advice, group advice, group decision.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'The decision tree’s questions (Exhibit 9.5) and three simple paths', ref: '§9.5 · slide 18',
          intro: 'The goal is a high-quality decision with adequate commitment and no undue delay. The tree always picks the <b>fastest (most autocratic) approach that still protects quality and commitment</b>. Do not memorize the whole tree; know the logic.',
          items: ['<b>QR</b> quality requirement · <b>CR</b> commitment requirement', '<b>LI</b> leader’s information · <b>ST</b> problem structure', '<b>CP</b> commitment probability if you decide alone · <b>GC</b> goal congruence · <b>CO</b> subordinate conflict · <b>SI</b> subordinate information', 'QR low, CR low: <b>AI</b> (just decide).', 'QR low, CR high: ask CP. Accepted anyway: <b>AI</b>; not: <b>GII</b>.', 'QR high, CR low, LI yes: <b>AI</b>.'] },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'info', title: 'Slides 17–18 add nothing beyond the text', body: 'Slide 17 matches the text’s three advantages and three problems; slide 18 gives the range AI, AII, CI, CII, GII and points to Exhibit 9.5. Nothing extra to memorize.' }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── RELATIONSHIP THEORIES ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'relationship', title: 'LMX, transactional & transformational leadership', ref: '§9.6 · slides 7, 19',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Leader–member exchange (LMX) theory', ref: '§9.6 · slide 19', def: 'Focuses on the <b>relationship between a leader and each individual employee</b>. A social-exchange approach: each leader–employee relationship is unique and differs in quality. Effective leadership results when leaders and employees develop <b>high-quality</b> exchange relationships. <b>LMX differentiation</b> is the variability in LMX quality among members of the same workgroup.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'High LMX vs low LMX', ref: '§9.6', head: ['', 'High LMX', 'Low LMX'],
          rows: [['Relationship', 'High mutual influence and obligation; trust, loyalty, open communication, respect', 'Low trust, respect, obligation and support'], ['Leader provides', 'Challenging tasks, latitude and discretion, resources, recognition', 'Less attention and latitude'], ['Employees do', 'Perform beyond their job descriptions (reciprocity)', 'Only what job descriptions demand']],
          after: 'High LMX goes with satisfaction, commitment, OCB, role clarity, performance and creativity, and lower turnover intentions. LMXD helps team performance when based on members’ <b>performance</b> and hurts when based on the leader’s <b>personal liking</b>.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Transactional leadership', ref: '§9.6 · slide 7', def: 'Leadership based on a <b>straightforward exchange</b> between leader and followers: leaders set goals and give direction and support, employees perform, and the leader rewards them. Two behaviours: <b>contingent reward</b> (rewarding followers for meeting goals) and <b>management by exception</b> (corrective action based on the results of transactions). <b>MBE-active</b> monitors and corrects as deviations happen; <b>MBE-passive</b> corrects only after mistakes occurred.',
          after: 'Most common style in organizations. Its positive effect on task performance and OCB comes mostly from <b>contingent reward</b>, not management by exception.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Transformational leadership: four dimensions (Bass)', ref: '§9.6 · slide 7',
          intro: 'Transformational leadership gives followers a <b>new vision</b> that instils true commitment and motivates performance beyond expectations. Such leaders are usually good at the transactional parts too.',
          items: ['<b>Intellectual stimulation</b>: getting people to think about problems in new ways; challenges assumptions.', '<b>Individualized consideration</b>: treating employees as individuals; one-on-one coaching and mentoring.', '<b>Inspirational motivation</b>: communicating appealing, values-based visions; optimism, high standards, meaning.', '<b>Charisma (idealized influence)</b>: the fourth and <b>by far the most important</b>; loyalty, devotion, identification with the leader and internalizing their values.'],
          after: 'The best leaders are <b>both transformational and transactional</b>. Transformational leadership is the most consistent predictor of leader effectiveness and works especially well in times of change.',
          mnem: '<b>Head, Hand, Horizon, Heart</b>: intellectual stimulation · individualized consideration · inspirational motivation · charisma.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Transactional vs transformational: text vs slide 7', src: 'lec', ref: 'slide 7', head: ['', 'Transactional', 'Transformational'],
          rows: [['Text', 'Contingent reward + management by exception', 'Four dimensions; new vision; beyond expectations'], ['Slide 7 adds', 'Manager-like; highly structured; order and routine; clear, time-bound tasks (manufacturing, sales); “rewards and punishments”', 'Arouses intense feelings; relies on personal sources of power; dynamic environments (tech start-ups, education)']],
          after: 'Use the text’s definition in written answers. “Manager-like,” the examples and “personal sources of power” are lecture wording only (power is Ch 12). The styles are complementary, not either/or.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Charisma is not “transformational”; MBE-passive is not laissez-faire', body: '<b>Charisma</b> is one of the four dimensions (idealized influence), not a synonym for transformational. <b>Individualized consideration</b> is not Ohio State consideration, and <b>contingent reward</b> is the same behaviour as in the leader-behaviours section. <b>MBE-passive</b> still steps in once something goes wrong; <b>laissez-faire</b> is avoiding or being absent from leadership altogether.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Slide 19 “alternative approaches”: where each is in the text', src: 'lec', ref: 'slide 19', head: ['Slide heading', 'In the 13th-ed chapter?'],
          rows: [['Leader–member exchange', 'Yes: §9.6'], ['<b>Developmental leadership</b>', '<b>No.</b> Heading only on the slide; the chapter has no section or definition for it'], ['Strategic leadership', 'Yes: §9.1'], ['Global leadership', 'Yes: §9.10'], ['Shared leadership', 'Yes: §9.1']] }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── POSITIVE LEADERSHIP ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'positive', title: 'Positive leadership', ref: '§9.7 · slide 20',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Positive leadership', ref: '§9.7 · slide 20', def: 'Leadership that focuses on <b>leader behaviours and interpersonal dynamics that increase followers’ confidence</b> and result in positive outcomes beyond task compliance (going beyond expectations, self-development, prosocial behaviour). Transformational leadership is also an example.' },
        { t: 'groups', title: 'Four theories of positive leadership', ref: '§9.7',
          groups: [
            { name: 'Empowering', items: ['Implementing conditions that <b>enable power to be shared</b>', 'Highlights work significance, gives participation and autonomy, expresses confidence, removes bureaucratic constraints', 'Produces psychological empowerment: <b>meaning, competence, self-determination, impact</b>'] },
            { name: 'Ethical', items: ['Normatively appropriate conduct (openness, fairness, honesty) through actions and relationships, promoted via two-way communication, reinforcement and decisions', 'Models honesty and care, makes ethics salient, rewards ethical and disciplines unethical behaviour', 'Promotion-focused helps trust; prevention-focused hurts it'] },
            { name: 'Authentic', items: ['Being true to oneself; consistency between values, beliefs and actions', 'Four behaviours: <b>self-awareness, relational transparency, balanced processing, internalized moral perspective</b>'] },
            { name: 'Servant', items: ['Serve first, lead second (Greenleaf); focus on followers’ needs and growth', 'Six characteristics: empowering and developing people, humility, authenticity, interpersonal acceptance, providing direction, stewardship'] }
          ],
          mnem: 'Empowerment: <b>Mean, Can, Choose, Matter</b>. Authentic: <b>Know, Show, Weigh, Walk</b>. Servant: <b>E-H-A-A-P-S</b> (Empowering, Humility, Authenticity, Acceptance, direction (Point), Stewardship).' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'The four positive styles overlap', body: '<b>Authentic</b> = true to your own values (inward). <b>Ethical</b> = modelling and promoting proper conduct in others (outward). <b>Servant</b> = serving followers first, and it includes authenticity as one of its six characteristics. <b>Empowering</b> = sharing power for a four-part psychological state; it is not the same as <b>participative</b> leadership (involvement in decisions).' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Slide 20 headings: in the chapter or not?', src: 'lec', ref: 'slide 20', head: ['Slide heading', 'In the 13th-ed Ch 9?'],
          rows: [['Empowering, ethical (“values based”), authentic, servant', 'Yes: §9.7'], ['Culture and leadership · Gender and leadership', 'Yes: §9.9 and §9.8'], ['<b>Emotional intelligence and leadership</b>', '<b>No</b> in Ch 9; EI is Ch 5 (Goleman model from Week 2). Heading only'], ['<b>Change leadership</b>', '<b>No.</b> Heading only (nearest point: transformational leadership suits times of change)'], ['<b>Leadership and sustainability</b>', '<b>No.</b> Heading only']] }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── GENDER / CULTURE / GLOBAL ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'gender-culture', title: 'Gender, culture & global leadership', ref: '§9.8–9.10',
      items: [
        { t: 'list', title: 'Gender and leadership: the findings', ref: '§9.8',
          items: ['Women tend to be more <b>participative / democratic</b> and lead more <b>transformationally</b> and with more <b>contingent reward</b>; men more with <b>management by exception</b> and <b>laissez-faire</b>.', 'The aspects where women exceed men are positively related to effectiveness; those where men exceed women are weak, negative or null.', 'Overall perceived effectiveness shows no difference once context is accounted for (masculine vs feminine organizations, level, who rates).', 'Yet women hold few senior roles in Canada.'] },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Glass ceiling, labyrinth and role congruity', ref: '§9.8', head: ['Idea', 'Meaning'],
          rows: [['<b>Glass ceiling</b>', 'One invisible barrier stopping women at the top'], ['<b>Labyrinth</b> (Eagly and Carli)', 'Many twists, turns and dead ends along the whole career; the shortage is the sum of all barriers'], ['<b>Role congruity theory</b>', 'Prejudice from an <b>incongruity</b> between perceived characteristics of women (communal) and requirements of leadership (agentic): “think manager, think male”'], ['<b>Agentic vs communal</b>', 'Agentic: assertion, control (dedicated, charismatic, intelligent, determined, aggressive). Communal: caring, compassion (caring, sensitive, honest, understanding, compassionate)']],
          after: 'A <b>universal mindset</b> (most people have high leadership potential) goes with less bias in evaluation and selection.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'The GLOBE project', ref: '§9.9', def: 'The most extensive study of culture and leadership: <b>170 researchers, 10 years, over 17,000 managers, 62 societal cultures</b>, grouped into 10 clusters. It used <b>nine cultural dimensions</b> (performance orientation, assertiveness, future orientation, humane orientation, institutional collectivism, in-group collectivism, gender egalitarianism, power distance, uncertainty avoidance) and found <b>six global leadership dimensions</b>.',
          after: 'The six: <b>charismatic / value-based, team-oriented, participative, humane-oriented, autonomous, self-protective</b>. Some attributes are universally positive (honest, decisive, motivational, dynamic), some universally negative (loner, irritable, egocentric, ruthless) and some depend on the culture. GLOBE’s nine are separate from Hofstede’s Ch 4 list.',
          mnem: '<b>“Charismatic Teams Plan Humane Actions Safely”</b>: Charismatic · Team · Participative · Humane · Autonomous · Self-protective.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Global leadership', ref: '§9.10', def: 'Having leadership capabilities to function effectively in different cultures and to cross language, social, economic and political borders. Needs a global mindset, tolerance for high ambiguity and cultural adaptability.',
          after: 'Gregersen, Morrison and Black’s four characteristics: <b>unbridled inquisitiveness, personal character, duality, savvy</b>. The most powerful development strategy is international work experience.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'info', title: 'The chapter’s answer: “It depends”', body: 'What style is best? A good leader uses many styles (consideration and structure are compatible; the best are both transformational and transactional), the <b>situation</b> determines what works, and leaders work through <b>mechanisms</b> (cognition, affect, behaviour). Use this to close any open-ended answer.' }
      ]
    },
    /* ─────────────── EQUATION ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'equation', title: 'The Leadership Effectiveness Equation (lecture)', ref: 'slides 22–29',
      lede: 'Lecture-only. The textbook has no formula; its nearest idea is Exhibit 9.10 (situation, style, mechanisms, effectiveness).',
      items: [
        { t: 'formula', title: 'Leader Effectiveness = L1 + L2 + GM + S', src: 'lec', ref: 'slide 22', f: 'Leader Effectiveness = L1 + L2 + GM + S',
          body: '<b>L1</b> leader traits · <b>L2</b> leader behaviours · <b>GM</b> group member characteristics · <b>S</b> situation. Trait theory is L1, the behaviour models are L2, LMX and the follower side of path–goal involve GM, and the situational theories are S. The “+” is shorthand: the theories show the terms interact (leader behaviour × situation).' },
        { t: 'groups', title: 'What sits under each term', src: 'lec', ref: 'slides 23–28',
          groups: [
            { name: 'L1 · Leader traits (23–24)', items: ['Physical, intellectual, personality', 'Intelligence, energy, self-confidence, dominance, motivation to lead, honesty and integrity, need for achievement', 'Big Five; locus of control, self-monitoring, self-esteem, positive / negative affectivity, proactive personality, self-efficacy, core self-evaluations (taught in Ch 2)'] },
            { name: 'L2 · Leader behaviours (25)', items: ['<b>Relationship-oriented</b> (“initiates consideration”): inspires, gives meaning, emotional support, promotes values, servant leadership, aligns and mobilizes', '<b>Task-oriented</b> (“initiates structure”): direction setting, high standards, hands-on guidance, frequent feedback, asks tough questions, customer orientation, adapts'] },
            { name: 'GM · Group member characteristics (26–27)', items: ['The same trait lists applied to followers: abilities, intelligence, motivation, energy, Big Five and the seven personality headings', 'Textbook support: path–goal (need for achievement, perceived ability) and LMX'] },
            { name: 'S · Situation (28)', items: ['<b>Internal:</b> power, politics, culture, subcultures, how clear / routine and how challenging tasks are, frustrating or dissatisfying jobs', '<b>External:</b> economic, competitors, customers, suppliers, industry associations, social, political, technological'] }
          ],
          mnem: '<b>Who, What, Whom, Where</b>: L1 = who the leader is, L2 = what the leader does, GM = whom the leader leads, S = where the leading happens.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: '“Relationship-oriented” appears twice, and “initiates consideration” again', body: 'Slide 25’s relationship-oriented <b>behaviours</b> (what a leader does) are not Fiedler’s relationship-oriented <b>leader</b> (a high-LPC attitude). And “initiates consideration” is again the slide’s phrase; the text says <b>consideration</b>. Only the internal-task items of S match the text (path–goal’s environmental factors); the external items are lecture-only.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'info', title: 'Slide 29: which component is most significant? (worked answer, not Dr. Kassaye’s)', body: 'Defensible pick: <b>S, the situation</b>, because every other term’s value depends on it. Trait theory fails mainly by ignoring the situation, Fiedler favours task at the extremes and relationship in the middle, path–goal says directive helps on ambiguous tasks and irritates on routine ones, and Vroom–Jago ties participation to quality, commitment and conflict. Then say what you can control: <b>L2</b>, since behaviours matter more than traits and can be learned. Acknowledge the counter-view: traits such as motivation to lead still shape who emerges as a leader.' }
      ]
    }
  ],

  quiz: [
    { q: 'Slide 3 contrasts “formal” and “informal” leaders. The textbook’s term for informal leadership is:', o: ['Shared leadership', 'Emergent leadership', 'Strategic leadership', 'Laissez-faire leadership'], a: 1, why: '<b>Informal = emergent</b>: someone with no formal status seen as a leader. Shared leadership is influence distributed across team members; strategic is about the organization’s future; laissez-faire is the avoidance of leadership.' },
    { q: 'Slide 10 labels one Ohio State behaviour “initiating consideration.” The textbook’s term is:', o: ['Initiating structure', 'Individualized consideration', 'Consideration', 'Supportive behaviour only'], a: 2, why: 'The textbook behaviour is simply <b>consideration</b>. “Initiating consideration” is the slide’s slip; individualized consideration is a transformational dimension. (Supportive is path–goal’s near-identical behaviour.)' },
    { q: 'A manager promises a bonus to any rep who beats quota, and pays it out only when quota is beaten. In the text, this is best described as:', o: ['Contingent reward behaviour, one of the two transactional behaviours', 'Individualized consideration', 'Management by exception, active', 'Leader punishment behaviour'], a: 0, why: 'Reward made <b>contingent on meeting goals</b> is contingent reward behaviour, the first of the two transactional behaviours (the other is management by exception).' },
    { q: 'A senior leader meets each of her managers one-on-one, learns their career goals and coaches them. Which transformational dimension is this?', o: ['Intellectual stimulation', 'Inspirational motivation', 'Charisma', 'Individualized consideration'], a: 3, why: '<b>Individualized consideration</b> = one-on-one attention, development and coaching (Hand). It differs from Ohio State consideration, which is a style toward the whole group.' },
    { q: 'A leader describes her least preferred co-worker in warm terms despite their poor performance. According to Fiedler she is:', o: ['Task oriented (high LPC)', 'Relationship oriented (high LPC)', 'Relationship oriented (low LPC)', 'Task oriented (low LPC)'], a: 1, why: 'A <b>favourable</b> description is a <b>high</b> LPC score, which means <b>relationship oriented</b>. Low LPC = task oriented.' },
    { q: 'A leader has poor relations with the team, an unstructured task and weak position power. Fiedler predicts the most effective leader will be:', o: ['High LPC (relationship oriented)', 'Whoever scores highest on consideration', 'Low LPC (task oriented)', 'Equally effective either way'], a: 2, why: 'Octant VIII, the <b>least favourable</b> situation. Task orientation (low LPC) is predicted at both extremes; relationship orientation (high LPC) in the middle.' },
    { q: 'Good leader–member relations, an unstructured task and weak position power (octant IV) are predicted by Fiedler to favour:', o: ['A relationship-oriented (high-LPC) leader', 'A task-oriented (low-LPC) leader', 'A directive leader under path–goal', 'No orientation; the situation is too unfavourable'], a: 0, why: 'Octant IV is <b>medium</b> favourability, where relationship orientation (high LPC) wins. Switching power to strong would give octant III (very favourable), which flips the prediction to task.' },
    { q: 'Under path–goal theory, which behaviour is most likely to lower satisfaction and leader acceptance for experienced employees doing clear, routine work?', o: ['Supportive leadership to offset a dull job', 'Achievement-oriented leadership with high goals', 'Praise that is tied to performance', 'Directive leadership'], a: 3, why: 'On clear, routine tasks directive leadership is <b>redundant</b> and reduces satisfaction and leader acceptance, and capable employees find it irritating. Participation is not useful either.' },
    { q: 'Which employee characteristics appear on slide 16 but not in the 13th-ed discussion of path–goal?', o: ['Need for achievement and perceived ability', 'Authoritarianism and locus of control', 'Task ambiguity and frustrating jobs', 'Preference for being told what to do'], a: 1, why: 'The text’s examples are need for achievement, preference for direction and perceived ability. <b>Authoritarianism and locus of control</b> (and “formal authority” as an environmental factor) are slide wording only.' },
    { q: 'What best distinguishes the Vroom–Jago model from path–goal’s participative behaviour?', o: ['Vroom–Jago prescribes a range of five strategies and how much participation to use for a given decision', 'Vroom–Jago is about supportive behaviour, not participation', 'Path–goal recommends participation in every situation', 'They are the same idea under two names'], a: 0, why: 'Path–goal: participative is one of four behaviours that suits some situations. Vroom–Jago: <b>AI, AII, CI, CII, GII</b> chosen with a decision tree on quality and commitment.' },
    { q: 'Quality of the decision hardly matters, but employee commitment matters a lot and would not follow if the manager decided alone. Vroom–Jago points to:', o: ['AI', 'CI', 'GII', 'AII'], a: 2, why: 'QR low, CR high, and commitment would not follow a solo decision (CP no): <b>GII</b>. If employees would accept it anyway, AI.' },
    { q: 'In which Vroom–Jago strategy does the leader gather information from employees but then decide alone, without asking them for ideas or evaluations?', o: ['AI', 'AII', 'CI', 'CII'], a: 1, why: '<b>AII</b>: employees are information providers only, not generating or evaluating alternatives. In CI / CII they act as advisers (one at a time / as a group).' },
    { q: 'A leader challenges long-held assumptions and asks followers to look at old problems in new ways. Which transformational dimension is this?', o: ['Charisma (idealized influence)', 'Inspirational motivation', 'Individualized consideration', 'Intellectual stimulation'], a: 3, why: '<b>Intellectual stimulation</b> (Head). Charisma is loyalty and devotion to the leader, and it is the most important of the four.' },
    { q: 'In the lecture’s Leadership Effectiveness Equation, “GM” stands for:', o: ['Group management practices', 'General motivation', 'Group member characteristics', 'Goal and mission'], a: 2, why: 'Leader Effectiveness = <b>L1</b> (leader traits) + <b>L2</b> (leader behaviours) + <b>GM</b> (group member characteristics) + <b>S</b> (situation). The equation is lecture-only.' },
    { q: 'Which slide 20 heading has no explanation in the 13th-ed Chapter 9?', o: ['Authentic leadership', 'Change leadership', 'Servant leadership', 'Gender and leadership'], a: 1, why: '<b>Change leadership</b> (like emotional intelligence and leadership, and leadership and sustainability) is a heading only. The other three are in §9.7–9.8.' }
  ],

  prompts: [
    { q: '<b>From last year’s Assignment #2 (Nov 2025, Dr. McAteer’s section), Q5:</b> (a) What are the components and lessons of House’s path–goal theory? (7 marks) (b) Use path–goal theory to explain how your leadership style would differ in each of the following situations (3 marks): (i) supervising incoming junior associates at a top law firm; (ii) leading an IT team through a major system outage; (iii) overseeing a recruitment event that your assistant manager has been planning for weeks. Support your answer with sound reasoning and examples.',
      name: '<b>House’s path–goal theory</b>: leader behaviours, situational factors (employee characteristics and environmental factors), outcomes, and the fit between them.',
      define: '<b>Path–goal theory</b> says the most important thing leaders do is clarify the paths to goals that matter to employees, which promotes job satisfaction, leader acceptance and effort. <b>Components:</b> (1) four <b>leader behaviours</b>: <b>directive</b> (schedules work, sets standards, says what is expected; like initiating structure), <b>supportive</b> (friendly, approachable, concerned; like consideration), <b>participative</b> (consults and considers opinions) and <b>achievement-oriented</b> (encourages high effort and expresses confidence); (2) <b>situational factors</b> in two classes: <b>employee characteristics</b> (need for achievement, preference for being told what to do, perceived task ability) and <b>environmental factors</b> (clear routine tasks, challenging ambiguous tasks, frustrating jobs); (3) <b>outcomes</b>: satisfaction, acceptance of the leader and effort. <b>Lessons:</b> no single behaviour is best; a behaviour helps only when it adds something the situation lacks and hurts when it is redundant; leaders should use the motivating parts of the job and offset the demotivating ones. The theory predicts satisfaction and acceptance better than performance. (Slide 16’s authoritarianism, locus of control and “formal authority” are not in the 13th ed.; I use the text’s factors.)',
      apply: '<b>(i) Law-firm junior associates: directive (with coaching), plus achievement-oriented.</b> Newcomers have low perceived task ability and face challenging, ambiguous legal work, and the text says low-ability employees appreciate directive leadership and coaching and that ambiguous tasks call for directive behaviour. I would set clear standards, explain how work is reviewed and show the path to partnership. Associates at a top firm are probably high achievers, so I would layer in <b>achievement-oriented</b> goals with expressed confidence, and add <b>supportive</b> behaviour because the long hours can be frustrating. As their ability grows, directive becomes redundant and irritating, so I would shift toward participative and achievement-oriented. <b>(ii) IT team in a major outage: directive, with brief participative input and supportive follow-up.</b> The task is challenging and ambiguous and the stakes are urgent, so employees appreciate someone who clarifies the path: who does what, priorities, timelines and communication. Participation is slow when time is short (the text’s emergency-room point), so I would consult only the technical experts who hold superior knowledge (quality), then decide. Highly capable engineers do not need step-by-step direction on the technical fix, so I would set the path, not micromanage it. Afterwards I would be <b>supportive</b>, because the job is stressful and frustrating. <b>(iii) Recruitment event your assistant manager has planned for weeks: participative and achievement-oriented, not directive.</b> The assistant manager is capable and knows the plan, so directive leadership would be redundant and irritating and would lower satisfaction and acceptance of me as leader. I would consult them (participative: ask for their view, consider their opinions), express confidence that they can deliver (achievement-oriented), and be available with supportive help if problems arise.',
      recommend: 'Diagnose first (employees, then environment), pick the behaviour that fills a gap, and avoid the one that would be redundant. My style should <b>move with the situation</b>: directive for new, low-ability people on ambiguous work (and briefly in a crisis), achievement-oriented and participative for capable people on a plan they own, supportive wherever the job is frustrating. Close with “it depends”, the chapter’s answer, and name the outcomes protected: satisfaction, leader acceptance and effort.',
      also: 'Link to Ohio State (directive = initiating structure, supportive = consideration); the Leadership Effectiveness Equation (GM and S); do not confuse path–goal’s participative behaviour with Vroom–Jago. If an answer should cite only the text’s factors, do not name authoritarianism or locus of control.' },
    { q: 'Priya has just been named lead of a temporary project team. Several members resent that she was picked over a senior colleague, the project goals are vague and she has no authority over pay or promotion. A year later she leads a well-established team with great trust, a clearly defined routine task and full formal authority. <b>Using Fiedler’s contingency theory, identify Priya’s situation each time, state which leader orientation should work best in each, and explain how LPC would be measured.</b>',
      name: '<b>Fiedler’s contingency theory</b>: LPC (high vs low), the three situational factors, the eight octants and the task-at-the-tails / relationship-in-the-middle prediction.',
      define: 'Fiedler says the link between leader orientation and group effectiveness depends on how favourable the situation is for exerting influence. <b>LPC:</b> the leader describes the co-worker with whom they had the most difficulty getting a job done. A favourable description (<b>high LPC</b>) means <b>relationship oriented</b>; an unfavourable one (<b>low LPC</b>) means <b>task oriented</b>. LPC is an attitude that reveals a personality trait, not the behaviours consideration or initiating structure. <b>Favourableness</b> depends on leader–member relations, task structure and position power, giving eight octants. Task orientation (low LPC) works best in very favourable (I–III) and very unfavourable (VIII) situations; relationship orientation (high LPC) in the middle (IV–VII).',
      apply: '<b>Year 1:</b> poor relations, unstructured task and weak position power is <b>octant VIII</b>, the least favourable. Fiedler predicts a <b>task-oriented (low-LPC)</b> leader is needed to get anything accomplished. <b>Year 2:</b> good relations, structured task and strong power is <b>octant I</b>, the most favourable. Employees are “ready” to be influenced, so a <b>task-oriented (low-LPC)</b> leader can get away with that style. Both ends point to task orientation, which shows the prediction is curvilinear, not a straight line. <b>Measuring LPC:</b> Priya would describe her least preferred co-worker; warm words mean high LPC (relationship), harsh words mean low LPC (task).',
      recommend: 'Because Fiedler treats LPC as a stable trait, the practical move is to match the leader to the situation or change the situation (for example, build relations or clarify the task). If Priya is high-LPC, Fiedler would warn that both of these extremes suit her less well, and a middle situation (such as poor relations but strong power, octants V–VII) would suit her best. Note the evidence contradicts the task prediction in octant II, so use the theory as a guide, not a law.',
      also: 'Contrast with path–goal (behaviour is flexible) and the slide 8 map (Fiedler = contingent · trait); high LPC does not mean high task focus.' },
    { q: 'A hospital director must decide (1) which colour to paint the staff lounge, (2) how to schedule weekend shifts, which every nurse cares about and must accept, and (3) how to respond to a sudden infection outbreak on a ward, where she is the one with the information and time is critical. <b>Use participative leadership and the Vroom–Jago model to recommend the degree of participation for each decision, and explain the advantages and problems of participation.</b>',
      name: '<b>Participative leadership</b> and the <b>Vroom–Jago</b> model (AI, AII, CI, CII, GII; quality and commitment).',
      define: '<b>Participative leadership</b> is involving employees in work-related decisions; it is relative (from asking opinions to employees deciding within limits) and is not abdication. <b>Advantages:</b> motivation and performance, quality, acceptance. <b>Problems:</b> time and energy, loss of power, lack of receptivity or knowledge. <b>Vroom–Jago</b> offers five strategies: <b>AI</b> (decide alone), <b>AII</b> (get information, then decide alone), <b>CI</b> (consult individually, then decide), <b>CII</b> (consult as a group, then decide), <b>GII</b> (group decides by consensus; leader chairs). The decision tree asks about quality requirement, commitment requirement, leader’s information, problem structure, commitment probability, goal congruence, conflict and employees’ information, and picks the fastest approach that protects quality and commitment.',
      apply: '<b>(1) Lounge colour:</b> quality and commitment barely matter (QR low, CR low), so <b>AI</b>: just decide. Handing over a trivial decision is the “loss of power” trap the text mentions. <b>(2) Weekend shifts:</b> any fair schedule is technically fine (QR low) but acceptance is essential (CR high) and nurses would not accept a decision imposed on them (CP no), so <b>GII</b>: let the nurses reach a consensus within limits. This uses participation’s acceptance advantage (fairness issues such as vacation schedules). <b>(3) Outbreak:</b> time is critical and the director holds the information, so <b>AI</b> (or AII if she needs a quick fact from ward staff). This is the “emergency room” case where the time-and-energy problem makes participation wrong.',
      recommend: 'Match participation to the decision: use AI where neither quality nor commitment is at stake or time is critical, GII where commitment is essential and would not follow, and consult (CI / CII) when employees hold knowledge she lacks. If time allows, more participation can develop staff, as long as quality and commitment are protected. Note that this is different from path–goal’s participative behaviour: Vroom–Jago tells her <i>how much</i> to involve people for <i>each decision</i>.',
      also: 'Tannenbaum–Schmidt continuum (Exhibit 9.4); path–goal participative behaviour; empowering leadership (not the same thing).' },
    { q: 'At a fast-growing software firm the CEO, Dana, shares a compelling vision, challenges teams to rethink old assumptions, personally mentors each director and is widely admired. She also sets clear targets, pays bonuses when they are hit and checks monthly reports for deviations. Some staff, however, say her favourites get the best projects. <b>Name the leadership styles Dana uses and their dimensions, evaluate the favouritism complaint using LMX, and recommend how she should lead.</b>',
      name: '<b>Transformational and transactional leadership</b> (four dimensions; contingent reward; management by exception) and <b>LMX</b> (LMX differentiation).',
      define: '<b>Transformational leadership</b> gives followers a new vision and motivates performance beyond expectations, through <b>intellectual stimulation</b>, <b>individualized consideration</b>, <b>inspirational motivation</b> and <b>charisma</b> (idealized influence, the most important). <b>Transactional leadership</b> is a straightforward exchange, built on <b>contingent reward</b> and <b>management by exception</b> (active or passive). <b>LMX</b> says each leader–employee relationship is unique in quality; <b>LMX differentiation</b> is the variation in quality among members of the same workgroup.',
      apply: '<b>Transformational:</b> the compelling vision is inspirational motivation; challenging assumptions is intellectual stimulation; mentoring each director is individualized consideration (one-on-one, not Ohio State consideration); the admiration and devotion reflect charisma. <b>Transactional:</b> clear targets with bonuses when hit is contingent reward; monthly checks for deviations is management by exception (active, since she monitors routinely). Dana is both, which the text says the best leaders are. <b>LMX:</b> “favourites get the best projects” describes high-LMX relationships (challenging tasks, latitude, resources) vs low-LMX ones (less attention). LMX differentiation can help team performance if it rests on members’ <b>performance</b> and clear roles, but hurts when it rests on the leader’s <b>personal liking</b>, creating relationship conflict.',
      recommend: 'Keep the transformational-plus-transactional mix. Make project assignments depend on visible performance and potential, explain the criteria, and extend high-quality exchange to more employees so differentiation is perceived as fair. Keep contingent reward; use management by exception actively so problems are caught early. Close with the chapter’s conclusion that effective leadership “depends” on the people and the situation.',
      also: 'Charisma is not a synonym for transformational; contingent reward = leader reward behaviour made contingent; MBE-passive is not laissez-faire; slide 7’s “personal sources of power” belongs to Ch 12.' }
  ]
});
